The Siken example shows how this works in practice. The stars become "little boats rowed out too far." The tenor is familiar enough: $\begin{aligned}
\text{tenor} &= \text{stars},\\
\text{vehicle} &= \text{boats rowed out too far}.\end{aligned}$ But the correspondence changes the whole symbolic regime. Stars, ordinarily grand and mythic, become intimate, lonely, late, vulnerable. The line does not merely offer a fresh comparison. It changes the emotional scale of the night sky.

