The lecture ends by pushing past craft into philosophical risk. Language has made Vuong’s life. It has given him livelihood, access, and enlargement of perception. Yet the same language can be captured by tyranny. Newspapers and radio are among the first targets of authoritarian control. The closing paradox must therefore remain intact: $\begin{aligned}
\text{language} &\Longrightarrow \text{power} + \text{livelihood} + \text{enlargement of perception},\\
\text{language} &\Longrightarrow \text{capture} + \text{tyranny} + \text{homogenization}.\end{aligned}$ Thomas Thistlewood’s library, the cultivated brutality of Nazi officers, and the other historical examples keep the lecture from becoming sentimental. At the same time, Harriet Beecher Stowe remains on the other side of the ledger. Literature sometimes does alter history. Vuong insists on both truths at once.

